diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 276a740..5ed9c00 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -58,6 +58,7 @@ Fields:
   - `includeTags`: array of tag slugs; a recipe must have all of them. Default none.
   - `excludeTags`: array of tag slugs; a recipe must have none of them. Default none.
   - `maxTotalTimeMinutes`: non-negative number or `null` (no limit). When set, a recipe with no parsable ISO-8601 `totalTime` is not eligible.
+  - `diet`: optional array of `"vegetarian"`, `"vegan"`, `"gluten-free"` (see "Diet rules" below). Any other value or a non-array gives `400 rules.diet: must be an array of "vegetarian", "vegan", "gluten-free"`.

 Eligible recipes are sorted by slug and assigned to the meal slots in rotation, so the same inputs give the same plan.

@@ -67,6 +68,22 @@ Response `201`:

 The ingredients of each planned recipe are copied into the database at creation time, so later changes in Mealie do not alter an existing plan or its shopping list.

+#### Diet rules (`rules.diet`)
+
+A recipe is dropped when any of its ingredients uses a food whose Mealie label is banned by one of the requested diets. Labels are compared case-insensitively (trimmed).
+
+| Diet | Banned food labels |
+|---|---|
+| `vegetarian` | `meat`, `fish` |
+| `vegan` | `meat`, `fish`, `dairy`, `egg` |
+| `gluten-free` | `gluten` |
+
+- The value is de-duplicated and sorted. Several diets combine: the banned labels are the union. The diet filter combines with the other rules (`includeTags`, `excludeTags`, `maxTotalTimeMinutes`); it is applied only to recipes that already pass them. If nothing is left, the answer is `409 no eligible recipes`.
+- Foods and their labels are read from Mealie `/api/foods` (paged). A food that is missing from that list, has no label, or an ingredient without a food name is never treated as a violation.
+- In the normalised rules stored with the plan, `diet` is present only when it is non-empty. An absent `diet` or an empty array (`[]`) stores no `diet` key.
+- **With a diet, a failure to fetch the foods gives `502 mealie unavailable: <reason>`** and nothing is stored (the foods request has a 10 s timeout). Without a diet the foods are fetched on a best-effort basis (2 s timeout): if that fails, the plan is still created with status 201, only without food labels, and a line is written to stderr.
+- The label of each food is copied into the stored ingredients at creation time (used by `groupBy=label`, below).
+
 ### GET /plans/:id

     curl http://localhost:3000/plans/3f0c...
@@ -79,6 +96,17 @@ The ingredients of each planned recipe are copied into the database at creation

 This reads only the database; it works even when Mealie is down.

+#### GET /plans/:id/shopping-list?groupBy=label
+
+    curl 'http://localhost:3000/plans/3f0c.../shopping-list?groupBy=label'
+    # 200 {"planId":"3f0c...","groups":[{"label":"Dairy","items":[{"food":"milk","quantity":1,"unit":"l"}]},{"label":"Other","items":[{"food":"salt","quantity":1,"unit":null}]}],"notes":["to taste"]}
+
+Instead of `items`, the response has `groups`: an array of `{label, items}`, where each item is `{food, quantity, unit}` in the usual order (food, then unit). Groups are sorted by label, with `Other` always last; there are no empty groups. `notes` is unchanged.
+
+- Grouping uses the label stored with the plan; labels that differ only in case or surrounding spaces share a group. Foods with no label, a blank label, or a label named `other` go to `Other`.
+- The only valid value is `label`. A repeated `groupBy`, any other value, or an empty value gives `400 groupBy: must be "label"`. An unknown plan id still gives 404 first. Without `groupBy` the response is the plain `items` form shown above.
+- **Limitation:** plans created before this feature, and plans created without labels (no diet and the foods lookup failed or the food has no label), have no stored labels, so all their items appear under `Other`. Labels are never fetched later, since this endpoint does not contact Mealie.
+
 ### Status codes

 | Status | When |
